"""How Long Does It Take to Close on a House in Indiana? (Nov 2026).

Facts and sources (checked Nov 3 2026):
- ICE Mortgage Monitor, May 2026 (published May 11 2026; March 2026 data): average purchase loan
  closed in 36.8 days in March (37.3 a year earlier, 38.1 in February), fastest since ICE began
  tracking in 2019; application to rate lock 11 days, lock to closing another 26 days; all
  origination types 38.2 days. ICE measures from loan application, not from accepted offer.
  No newer ICE time-to-close figure was found.
- Chase, "How long does it take to close on a house?" (Jan 12 2026): about 43 days from accepted
  offer to keys on average; cash buyers "can often close in just a couple of weeks."
- CFPB: Loan Estimate within three business days of receiving the application; Closing Disclosure
  received at least three business days before closing. Reg Z 1026.19(f)(1)(ii)(A) and official
  interpretation: for this timing "business day" = all calendar days except Sundays and legal public
  holidays (Saturdays count). 1026.19(f)(2)(ii): new three-business-day wait if the APR becomes
  inaccurate, the loan product changes, or a prepayment penalty is added.
- 876 IAC 8-2-2(a): listing broker deposits earnest money in escrow, or with the party the purchase
  agreement names, on or before the next two banking days after final acceptance.
- VA Appraisal Fees and Timeliness table (effective May 1 2026): Indiana 8 business days; Allen,
  Hamilton, Lake and Marion counties 7. Timeliness starts the first business day after assignment.
- HUD Handbook 4000.1: FHA appraisals check HUD's minimum property requirements; required repairs
  can delay closing (framed as "may need", per underwriter).
- Freddie Mac UAD page / our UAD 3.6 post: redesigned appraisal report required from Nov 2 2026.
- HomeLight (updated Sep 28 2026): Indiana does not require an attorney at closing.
- Federal Reserve holidays (K.8): Nov 26 2026, Dec 25 2026, Jan 1 2027, Jan 18 2027.
- Existing site posts: financed under-contract period typically 30-45 days; inspection in the
  first week or two; final walk-through usually within a day or two of closing.
"""

ICE = "https://mortgagetech.ice.com/resources/data-reports/may-2026-mortgage-monitor"
CHASE = "https://www.chase.com/personal/mortgage/education/buying-a-home/how-long-to-close-on-a-house"
VA = "https://www.benefits.va.gov/HOMELOANS/documents/docs/va-appraisal-fees-and-timeliness-table.pdf"

BODY = """
 <p>Once your offer is accepted, the next question is always the same: when do we get the keys? In Central Indiana, most financed purchases close about 30 to 45 days after the offer is accepted, and cash deals can move much faster. Here&rsquo;s what happens in between, roughly week by week, and what tends to push a closing date back. If you&rsquo;re earlier in the process, start with our guide to <a href="/services/home-buying-process/">the home buying process</a>.</p>

 <h2 id="short-answer">The Short Answer: About 30 to 45 Days With a Mortgage</h2>
 <p>Your purchase agreement sets the closing date, and most financed purchases in Central Indiana are written to close about <strong>30 to 45 days</strong> after acceptance. National data lines up with that. According to ICE&rsquo;s May 2026 Mortgage Monitor, the average purchase loan closed in <strong>36.8 days in March 2026</strong>, down from 37.3 days a year earlier and the fastest monthly average since ICE began tracking it in 2019. The typical loan took 11 days to go from application to rate lock and another 26 days from lock to closing.</p>
 <p>One caveat: ICE counts from the loan application, not the accepted offer. Most buyers make a formal application right after going under contract, so the two clocks are close but not identical. Chase, for example, puts the average at about 43 days from accepted offer to keys (January 2026).</p>

 <h2 id="timeline">A Typical Indiana Closing Timeline</h2>
 <p>Every contract sets its own deadlines, so treat these as typical ranges for a financed purchase, not rules:</p>
 <div style="overflow-x:auto;">
 <table class="data-table">
 <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Typical timing</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>
 <tbody>
 <tr><td>Offer accepted</td><td>Day 0</td><td>The purchase agreement is signed and the closing date is set. Your agent sends the contract to your lender and the title company.</td></tr>
 <tr><td><a href="/blog/earnest-money-in-indiana/">Earnest money</a></td><td>Within 2 banking days</td><td>Indiana rules require the listing broker to deposit it in escrow, or with whoever the contract names, within two banking days of final acceptance.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Loan application</td><td>Days 1&ndash;3</td><td>You complete the formal application. Your lender must send a Loan Estimate within three business days.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Home inspection</td><td>Week 1&ndash;2</td><td>The inspection usually happens in the first week or two, followed by any repair requests within your contract&rsquo;s deadlines.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Appraisal</td><td>Weeks 1&ndash;3</td><td>Your lender orders the appraisal to confirm the home supports the price.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Title search and survey</td><td>Weeks 1&ndash;3</td><td>The title company checks ownership and liens and issues a title commitment. A survey is ordered if your lender requires one or you want one.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Underwriting</td><td>Weeks 2&ndash;5</td><td>The underwriter verifies your income, assets, appraisal and title, clears any conditions and issues the clear to close.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Closing Disclosure</td><td>3+ business days before closing</td><td>You receive your final loan terms and costs to compare with your Loan Estimate.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Final walk-through</td><td>A day or two before closing</td><td>You confirm the home is in the condition you agreed to buy.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Closing</td><td>About day 30&ndash;45</td><td>You sign, your funds are wired, and you get the keys.</td></tr>
 </tbody>
 </table>
 </div>
 <p>A few of those steps deserve more detail. Your contract&rsquo;s <a href="/blog/contingencies-in-an-indiana-purchase-agreement/">contingencies</a> come with firm deadlines, and the <a href="/blog/home-inspection-under-contract-what-to-expect-indianapolis/">home inspection</a> is usually the first one. The <a href="/blog/home-inspections-appraisals-indianapolis/">appraisal</a> runs in parallel, and if it comes in below the price, our guide to <a href="/blog/what-to-do-when-your-appraisal-comes-in-low-indiana/">a low appraisal in Indiana</a> walks through your options. On the title side, the title company searches the public record and issues a <a href="/glossary/title-commitment/">title commitment</a>, and some buyers or lenders add a survey (here are the <a href="/blog/property-survey-types-buying-home-indianapolis/">survey types</a>).</p>
 <p>In Indiana, the closing itself usually takes place at a title company. The state doesn&rsquo;t require an attorney at a residential closing, although you can hire one if your situation is complicated. For what happens at the table, see our <a href="/blog/final-walk-through-checklist-indiana/">final walk-through checklist</a> and <a href="/blog/closing-day-what-to-expect-buying-a-home-indianapolis/">what to expect on closing day</a>.</p>

 <h2 id="cash">Closing With Cash</h2>
 <p>Paying cash removes the slowest parts of the process: there&rsquo;s no loan application, no lender-required appraisal and no underwriting. That&rsquo;s why cash buyers can often close in about <strong>a couple of weeks</strong>, as Chase notes. The limits are the title search, any inspection period you negotiated, and the seller&rsquo;s own moving timeline. Even with cash, we recommend an inspection and an owner&rsquo;s title insurance policy, and you&rsquo;ll need proof of funds and good funds for closing, usually a verified wire.</p>

 <h2 id="loan-types">FHA, VA and Conventional Loans</h2>
 <ul>
 <li><strong>Conventional loans.</strong> Starting November 2, 2026, appraisals on most conventional loans use Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac&rsquo;s redesigned report, which asks for more detail. Appraisals may take a little longer while appraisers adjust. Our <a href="/blog/uad-3-6-appraisal-changes-indiana/">UAD 3.6 explainer</a> covers what changes.</li>
 <li><strong>FHA loans.</strong> The appraiser also checks the home against HUD&rsquo;s minimum property requirements. If repairs are required, they may need to be finished, and sometimes re-inspected, before the loan can close, which can add days or weeks. See our guide to <a href="/blog/fha-loans-in-indiana-requirements-and-benefits/">FHA loans in Indiana</a>.</li>
 <li><strong>VA loans.</strong> The VA assigns the appraiser. Its timeliness standard in Indiana is <strong>8 business days</strong>, or 7 in Marion, Hamilton, Allen and Lake counties (VA table effective May 1, 2026), counted from the first business day after the assignment. More in our <a href="/blog/va-loans-in-indiana-a-complete-guide-for-veterans/">VA loan guide</a>.</li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="delays">What Delays a Closing</h2>
 <ul>
 <li><strong>A low appraisal.</strong> If the value comes in under the price, you, the seller or both need to agree on a fix, and that negotiation takes time.</li>
 <li><strong>Repair negotiations.</strong> Back-and-forth over inspection items, or waiting on contractor quotes, can stretch past the original deadlines.</li>
 <li><strong>Title problems.</strong> An unreleased old mortgage, a lien, an estate that hasn&rsquo;t been settled or a boundary question has to be cleared before the title company can insure the sale.</li>
 <li><strong>Paperwork and credit changes.</strong> Missing documents slow underwriting, and a new car loan, credit card or job change before closing can force the lender to re-qualify you.</li>
 <li><strong>Changes to the loan.</strong> If the APR becomes inaccurate, the loan product changes or a prepayment penalty is added after you get your Closing Disclosure, federal rules require a corrected one and a new three-business-day wait. For this rule, Saturdays count as business days, but Sundays and federal holidays don&rsquo;t.</li>
 <li><strong>Holidays.</strong> Banks and many lenders and title companies close on federal holidays, including Thanksgiving (November 26, 2026), Christmas (December 25), New Year&rsquo;s Day (January 1, 2027) and Martin Luther King Jr. Day (January 18, 2027). A late-December closing needs extra cushion.</li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="on-schedule">How to Keep Your Closing on Schedule</h2>
 <ul>
 <li><strong>Get fully pre-approved before you offer,</strong> not just pre-qualified. Here&rsquo;s <a href="/blog/pre-approval-vs-pre-qualification-indianapolis/">the difference</a>.</li>
 <li><strong>Answer your lender fast.</strong> Send requested documents the same day if you can, and don&rsquo;t open new credit or change jobs until after closing.</li>
 <li><strong>Book the inspection right away,</strong> so you have time to negotiate inside your deadline.</li>
 <li><strong>Line up homeowners insurance early.</strong> Your lender needs proof of coverage before it will clear you to close.</li>
 <li><strong>Read your Closing Disclosure the day it arrives</strong> and ask about anything that doesn&rsquo;t match your Loan Estimate.</li>
 <li><strong>Protect your wire.</strong> Call your title company at a number you already know to confirm wiring instructions, and never trust changes that arrive by email. Our <a href="/services/buyer-closing-checklist/">buyer closing checklist</a> and <a href="/glossary/closing-wire-fraud/">wire fraud explainer</a> cover the details.</li>
 </ul>

 <h2 id="our-take">Our Take</h2>
 <p>For most Central Indiana buyers with a mortgage, a 30- to 45-day closing is realistic, and a well-organized deal can land at the shorter end. Cash can be faster, but the title search and your inspection period still set the pace. The closings that slip are usually the ones where a deadline gets missed or a document sits in an inbox. Our team tracks every date for you, from earnest money to the walk-through. <a href="/schedule/">Book a call with our team</a> or read more about <a href="/services/home-buying-process/">how buying a home works</a>.</p>
"""

